        script: "Woman: Good afternoon. I would like some information about your evening courses, please.\nMan: Of course. Which course are you interested in?\nWoman: The one called English for University Study. I am planning to apply for a master's degree next year, and I need to improve my academic writing.\nMan: That is a very popular course. It starts on Monday the fifteenth of September, and classes are held on Tuesdays and Thursdays, from half past six to half past eight in the evening.\nWoman: Two evenings a week. How long does the course last?\nMan: Twelve weeks in total. By the end, most students can write a clear academic essay and understand university lectures much better.\nWoman: And how much does it cost?\nMan: The full price is two hundred and forty pounds. You can pay it all at once, or in three instalments of eighty pounds if that is easier.\nWoman: Instalments would help. Who teaches the course?\nMan: Ms Parker. She has taught academic English for fifteen years, and students say she explains grammar very clearly. Your class will be in Room 4B, on the fourth floor.\nWoman: Is there a test before I start?\nMan: Yes, a short level test on the first evening, so we can place you in the right group. Please bring some photo ID and a bank card if you want to set up the instalments.\nWoman: And do I need to buy any books for the course?\nMan: Only one coursebook, which costs fourteen pounds, although we can lend it to you for a returnable deposit of ten pounds if you prefer. All the other materials, including practice papers and writing worksheets, are included in the price. You will also receive a password for our student website, where Ms Parker posts extra practice every week.\nWoman: That is good value. How many students will be in my class?\nMan: Never more than fourteen, so the teacher has time to mark everyone's writing each week and discuss it with them individually.\nWoman: Is there parking near the college in the evening?\nMan: Yes, the student car park behind the main building is free after six o'clock, and the number twelve bus stops right outside the gate.\nWoman: One more question. Can I use the library after class?\nMan: Yes, the library stays open until nine o'clock, and course students can borrow up to four books at a time. At the end of the course you also receive a certificate.\nWoman: That sounds perfect. I will enrol online tonight.\nMan: Now, before we finish this open evening, please welcome Dr Ahmed, who will give you three short pieces of advice about studying. Dr Ahmed?\nLecturer: Thank you. First, study in short blocks. Twenty-five minutes of real concentration is better than two hours of tired reading. After each block, stand up, walk around and rest your eyes. Second, test yourself. Closing the book and trying to remember is far more powerful than reading your notes again and again. And third, never underestimate sleep. The night before an exam, get a good night's sleep instead of studying until morning. Your memory works while you sleep, so a rested brain remembers more than an exhausted one. Finally, study with other people sometimes. Explaining an idea to a friend is one of the fastest ways to discover what you do not yet understand, and a study partner makes it far harder to give up in week three. Small habits, done daily, beat heroic efforts done rarely. Good luck to all of you.",
