            title: "The Town That Stopped Throwing Things Away",
            text: "Twenty years ago, Greenfield was an ordinary town of 15,000 people, set among green hills, and it was sending 400 truckloads of rubbish to a landfill every year while the council paid rising fees for every load. The change began with a simple problem. The old landfill near the town was full, and building a new one would cost millions. Instead of paying, the council asked a different question: what if almost everything we throw away could be used again? The first step was education. Volunteers visited every home with a set of coloured boxes and a simple chart. Glass went into one box, metal into another, paper into a third. Food scraps were collected separately and turned into compost for gardens and farms. Within a year, the town was sorting its waste into twenty-four different categories, and schoolchildren were teaching their grandparents the rules. Not everyone found the new system easy. Elderly residents worried about carrying heavy boxes, so the council arranged helpers on collection days, and for the first months a few black bags still appeared beside the road on dark evenings. The town's schools became part of the programme as well. Each class adopted one category of waste for a term, weighing it, charting it and presenting the results to parents, so the project became a lesson in mathematics and science as much as in tidiness. Shops played a central part too. Local stores agreed to take back packaging, and customers received a small discount when they returned bottles and jars. A repair cafe opened in the old library, where volunteers fixed broken bicycles, radios and chairs for free. A second-hand exchange was set up in the market square, so furniture and clothes could pass directly from one family to another instead of going into a bin. The results surprised even the council. After five years, the amount of waste sent to landfill had fallen by 85 per cent. The compost programme supplied local farmers, who reported healthier soil and lower spending on chemical fertilisers. The town also saved money: although the sorting centre employed twelve people, the council no longer paid landfill fees, and it earned income by selling metal, glass and good-quality plastic to recycling companies. The system is not perfect. Some residents complain that sorting takes time and that the rules are complicated. A small amount of waste, such as certain medical items, still has to be burned for safety. Visitors from other towns sometimes leave disappointed, because Greenfield has no secret technology to copy. Its success rests on habits, not machines: people thinking before they throw something away, and a community that decided waste was a design fault, not a fact of life.",
