        script: "Woman: Good morning. I am writing an article for our school magazine about recycling, and I wonder if I can ask you a few questions.\nMan: Of course. Welcome to the Riverside Recycling Centre. We opened eight years ago, and now we receive waste from about thirty thousand homes.\nWoman: What materials do you accept here?\nMan: Glass, metal, paper and cardboard, and most types of plastic. The one thing we cannot take is plastic bags, because they get stuck in our machines. People should return those to supermarkets instead.\nWoman: What happens to the glass?\nMan: It is crushed, melted and made into new bottles. A glass bottle can be recycled again and again, almost forever, and it saves a lot of energy compared with making new glass from sand.\nWoman: And what about old phones and computers?\nMan: Electronic waste goes to a special building behind this one. Phones contain valuable metals like gold and copper, so it is worth recovering them. Last year we collected eleven tonnes of electronic waste.\nWoman: Eleven tonnes! Is the centre open every day?\nMan: We are open from eight in the morning until six in the evening, Monday to Saturday. We are closed on Sundays. In spring we also run free workshops where children learn to repair toys instead of throwing them away.\nWoman: That is a lovely idea. Before I go, can I ask about batteries and old paint? People at school always ask me.\nMan: Batteries should never go in the household bin. You can leave them in the small red box by our entrance, and most supermarkets collect them too. Old paint is more difficult: keep it dry, and bring it here on the first Saturday of the month, when we accept chemicals safely.\nWoman: And what is the most common mistake people make?\nMan: Putting dirty food containers in the recycling. A quick rinse is enough, but if a pizza box is soaked in oil, it belongs with general waste, because one dirty load can spoil a whole batch of paper.\nWoman: Thank you very much.\nMan: Now let me introduce our education officer, who will finish with a few words about saving water.\nLecturer: Thank you. People are often surprised to learn how much water a normal family wastes. A dripping tap can waste fifteen litres a day, so fix your taps. A short shower uses about thirty-five litres, while a full bath uses eighty, so choose the shower when you can. And never leave the tap running while you brush your teeth — that simple habit can save six litres every minute. Small actions, repeated by millions of people, protect our rivers and reservoirs. In the garden, collect rainwater in a barrel for your plants instead of using the hose, and water early in the morning, when less is lost to the sun. And when you buy a new washing machine or dishwasher, choose one with a high efficiency rating; over its lifetime, it will pay for itself in saved water and energy.",
