          sample: "Environmental problems such as climate change are so enormous that many people question whether one person's behaviour matters at all. This essay will examine both sides of the argument before giving my own view. On one side, it is easy to see why individuals feel powerless. A single family's recycling is nothing compared with the pollution produced by one factory, and a person who cycles to work shares the road with thousands of cars. In addition, the most serious decisions, about energy policy, forests and oceans, are taken by governments and large companies. From this perspective, expecting individuals to save the planet through small daily habits seems unrealistic and unfair. On the other hand, individual actions add up in powerful ways. Every product a company sells exists because individuals choose to buy it, so when millions of consumers refuse plastic packaging or waste less food, businesses change quickly. Individuals also influence each other: a neighbour who installs solar panels or grows vegetables makes those choices normal for a whole street. Finally, governments themselves are made of voters, and public concern, expressed by individuals, is what pushes politicians to pass environmental laws. In my opinion, it is wrong to say that individuals can do nothing, but equally wrong to pretend that personal habits alone are enough. Real progress happens when individual action and government policy push in the same direction: citizens create the demand for change, and governments turn that demand into rules and investment. To conclude, individual actions do make a genuine difference, not because one person can solve the crisis, but because societies are simply collections of individuals, and no lasting change has ever begun anywhere else."
