        title: "Buying a Laptop — and a Talk on Children and Screen Time",
        script: "Woman: Good afternoon. I am looking for a laptop for my daughter. She is starting university in October and needs it for writing essays and attending online lectures.\nMan: Then I would recommend this model. It has a fourteen-inch screen, which is big enough to work comfortably but light enough to carry to the library. It only weighs one point three kilograms.\nWoman: That sounds good. My daughter's old laptop was very heavy. How long does the battery last?\nMan: About ten hours of normal use, so she can work all day without the charger. The memory is five hundred and twelve gigabytes of storage, which is plenty for documents and photographs.\nWoman: And the price?\nMan: It is four hundred and ninety-nine pounds, but this week students get a ten per cent discount, and we include a free laptop bag.\nWoman: Is there a guarantee?\nMan: Yes, two years for parts and labour, and you can return it within thirty days if she is not happy. We also install the word-processing software free of charge before you take it home.\nWoman: Perfect. I will take it.\nMan: A good choice. While I prepare it, let me mention the keyboard and screen, as students always ask. The keys are large and quiet for long essays, and there is a port on the left side for connecting a bigger screen or a projector in the library. We also offer a student protection plan for twenty-five pounds a year, which covers accidental damage, including drinks spilled on the keyboard, although most students do not take it in the first year.\nWoman: Can she pay in instalments if she prefers?\nMan: Yes, over six months at no extra cost, if she shows her student card at the till.\nMan: Now, while we prepare your laptop, let me play you a short talk from our community technology evening, given by a children's doctor, about screen time.\nLecturer: Good evening. Parents often ask me how much screen time is safe for children. There is no single magic number, but the quality of what children watch and do matters as much as the quantity. Watching a documentary together and discussing it is very different from a child sitting alone with fast, noisy games for three hours. My practical advice is simple. Keep screens out of bedrooms at night, because sleep is when the brain grows and organises memory. Agree family rules together, rather than simply taking the phone away, so that children understand the reasons. And finally, look at your own behaviour: if parents are always on their phones, children will copy them. Technology is a tool; families must teach children to be its master, not its servant. One final thought: agree on a place in the home where everyone, adults included, leaves their phone during meals. Conversation is a skill, just like reading, and it only grows with practice. If children see that some moments of the day are protected from screens, they learn that attention is something we choose where to give.",
